<h2>2026-08-15 — Documentation: one shared page header, META-driven, with a Project link</h2>

<p><strong>Requested by:</strong> the user liked the header on <code>.md</code> doc pages
(<code>Documentation/MarkdownViewer.tt</code>: title + <em>Current Site</em> + <em>User Role</em> +
<em>Last Updated</em> + <em>Documentation Home</em> + <em>Suggest Edit</em>) and wanted the same on
<code>.tt</code> doc pages — plus a link to the project the documentation belongs to, derived from META.</p>

<h3>What changed</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>New shared include</strong> <code>root/admin/documentation/_doc_header.tt</code> — renders the
        consistent header for <em>both</em> <code>.tt</code> and <code>.md</code> doc pages. All values are
        data-driven from the stash (META block + request context); nothing is hardcoded except labels.</li>
    <li><strong>Project link</strong> — when a doc declares <code>project_id</code> (and optionally
        <code>project_code</code>) in its META (or, for <code>.md</code>, a
        <code>project_id = "NNN"</code> / <code>project_code = "CODE"</code> line), the header shows
        <code>Project: #NNN (CODE)</code> linking to <code>/project/details?project_id=NNN</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Wired into</strong> <code>DocumentationTtTemplate.tt</code> (replacing its hand-rolled
        last-updated line) and <code>MarkdownViewer.tt</code> (replacing its inline header), so every new
        <code>.tt</code> doc gets the header by <code>INCLUDE</code> and existing docs pick it up on next touch.</li>
    <li><strong>Controller wiring</strong> — <code>Documentation.pm</code> now passes
        <code>project_id</code>/<code>project_code</code> (parsed from META) and <code>last_updated</code> into the
        stash for both file types. <code>ScanMethods.pm _extract_md_metadata</code> now parses
        <code>project_id</code>/<code>project_code</code> from <code>.md</code> docs
        (RULE P1 — a plan must be linked to a project).</li>
    <li><strong>Theme compliance</strong> — new <code>.doc-page-header*</code> classes added to
        <code>root/static/css/components/documentation.css</code>; the include uses only
        <code>.doc-*</code> / <code>var(--*)</code> — no inline <code>style=</code>, no hardcoded hex.</li>
    <li><strong>First "touched" files</strong> carrying the linkage:
        <code>DocumentationTtTemplate.tt</code> META → <code>project_id=47</code> (Doc), and
        <code>TodoListPageDisplayTuningPlan.md</code> → <code>project_id=240</code> (TODOLIST-UI).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Verification</h3>
<ul>
    <li>All three templates parse cleanly (Template::Document parse).</li>
    <li>Standalone render of the include with <code>doc_project_id=240</code> outputs
        <code>Project: #240 (TODOLIST-UI)</code> → <code>/project/details?project_id=240</code>.</li>
    <li>Parsers verified on the real files: <code>.md</code> → <code>project_id=240</code>,
        <code>.tt</code> → <code>project_id=47</code>.</li>
    <li>Live admin view: hard-refresh a doc page to confirm the header + Project link render
        (Guest is role-gated by design, so the link is only visible when logged in).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Files</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>root/admin/documentation/_doc_header.tt</code> (new)</li>
    <li><code>root/Documentation/DocumentationTtTemplate.tt</code></li>
    <li><code>root/Documentation/MarkdownViewer.tt</code></li>
    <li><code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Documentation.pm</code></li>
    <li><code>lib/Comserv/Controller/Documentation/ScanMethods.pm</code></li>
    <li><code>root/static/css/components/documentation.css</code></li>
    <li><code>root/Documentation/TodoListPageDisplayTuningPlan.md</code></li>
</ul>
